    python3 bldg/build_bldg.py      # rewrites gen/bldg-data.js (meta only)

2026-08-24 — SHIP THE FLAG CONTRACT INSTEAD OF REMEMBERING IT.

FLAG_BLOCKYEAR (bit 3) and FLAG_NOYEAR (bit 4) have ridden in the `u` byte
since this file was written, and NOTHING IN THE PAYLOAD SAID SO. Every consumer
that wanted the numbers hand-copied them out of a source comment — which is
exactly how "12,594", a PARCEL count of the roll's 1900 dump, came to be
published on the page as a FOOTPRINT count and repeated in nine places.

`meta.yearFlags` now ships the bit, the mask, the count, what the flag means and
how the thing is drawn, measured on the run that wrote the file. A consumer that
reads it cannot go stale; a consumer that copies it can, and did.

Also: the bare literal 1906 in the FLAG_NOYEAR branch is now
UNDATED_PLACEHOLDER_YEAR, with a comment saying what it is — a placeholder that
keeps the stream fixed-width, not a date, and not to be read as one. (0 was
considered and rejected: it decodes to 1849 through the year0 offset, so it
would be a different wrong year rather than none. Changing the sentinel is a
cross-file contract change and belongs with the renderer.)

Verified: every payload array — cnt, vx, vy, xi, xn, h, yr, u — is BYTE-IDENTICAL
before and after. This changes `meta` and nothing else. The shipped counts are
checked against the `u` byte itself: bit 3 set on 20,571, bit 4 on 2,916, use
class after masking maxes at 6 of 6.

NOTE FOR WHOEVER OWNS website/src/lib/city/time.ts: the figures landed there as
20,662 / 2,825 are now 20,571 / 2,916 — the block-key fix moved 91 footprints
from the block-p25 cohort into the no-year cohort, correctly, because they sit
on no published block. The total, 23,487 of 166,166, is unchanged and cannot
move. Read them from meta.yearFlags rather than copying them a second time.

